doc -- a simple documentation function
**************************************

  * Usage: 
        doc s
        document s
  * Inputs:
      * s, a *note string: (Macaulay2Doc)String,, a structured source for the
        documentation, either as a string or a filename
  * Consequences:
      * Documentation is created and stored so that after *note installPackage:
        (Macaulay2Doc)installPackage, is used, *note help: (Macaulay2Doc)help,
        and *note viewHelp: (Macaulay2Doc)viewHelp, can be used to access the
        corresponding documentation node(s)

Description
===========

The string s contains a series of sections describing the documentation to be
produced. The first line of each section contains only a keyword that specifies
the purpose of that section. The keywords must all be indented to the same
level, and everything else must be further indented. Lines beginning with white
space followed by two hyphens are ignored and can be used for commenting.

For backwards compatibility, the function multidoc is also a synonym for doc.

Template for documentation strings
==================================

The variable docTemplate is a *note String: (Macaulay2Doc)String,, which can be
cut and pasted into a text file, to be used as a template for writing
documentation for functions and other objects in a package.

+-----------------+
|doc ///          |
|Node             |
|  Key            |
|  Headline       |
|  Usage          |
|  Inputs         |
|  Outputs        |
|  Consequences   |
|    Item         |
|  Description    |
|    Text         |
|    Tree         |
|    Code         |
|    Pre          |
|    Example      |
|    CannedExample|
|  ExampleFiles   |
|  Acknowledgement|
|  Contributors   |
|  References     |
|  Caveat         |
|  SeeAlso        |
|  Subnodes       |
|///              |
+-----------------+

Description of keywords allowed in documentation strings
========================================================

The only keyword which is always required is *note Key: (Macaulay2Doc)Key,.
Other valid keywords are:



  * *note Key: (Macaulay2Doc)Key, -- key of a documentation node

    Each line in this section is evaluated as a Macaulay2 expression to yield a
    documentation key. If the key is a string, for instance the title of a
    tutorial, it must be surrounded by quotation marks. Examples for
    documentation keys are given in *note document(...,Key=>...):
    (Macaulay2Doc)Key,.
  * *note Headline: (Macaulay2Doc)Headline, -- headline of a documentation node

    The body of this section should be a single line description of the node.
  * *note Usage: (Macaulay2Doc)Usage, -- ways to use a function in a
    documentation node

    The lines of this section present alternative usages of the key.
  * *note Inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)Inputs, -- inputs for a function in a
    documentation node
    *note Outputs: (Macaulay2Doc)Outputs, -- outputs for a function in a
    documentation node

    The text in these sections is divided into subsections, each of which
    corresponds to one input or output value. The first line of each subsection
    contains the name of the variable corresponding to it, as referred to in
    the usage line, a colon, and the type of the variable. The name of the
    variable must be a nonempty string of alphanumeric characters or quotation
    marks.  For example, the first line might be s2:String.  The variable name
    and type are both optional.

    For optional inputs, one puts the name of the option, a double arrow, and
    the expected type of value; for example, the first line might be
    Precision=>ZZ. Subsequent lines of the subsection, indented further,
    describe the variable and may include markup.
  * *note Consequences: (Macaulay2Doc)Consequences, -- side-effects of a
    function in a documentation node

    Each subsection of the this section should start with the keyword Item; the
    text in each item may include markup.
  * Description

    The text in this section occurs in subsections with keywords:

      * Text -- prose containing markup or tex

        The text in this subsection can be separated into paragraphs with blank
        lines. A paragraph, when rendered in HTML, is typically enclosed in an
        HTML <p> tag, except if its top level markup is an instance of *note
        HypertextContainer: (Text)HypertextContainer, or *note
        HypertextParagraph: (Text)HypertextParagraph,.
      * Tree -- classify documentation nodes under headings

        The lines in this section beginning with -  are treated as subsection
        headlines, and may contain Macaulay2 code or *note Hypertext:
        (Text)Hypertext, wrapped in @...@, while lines beginning with *  or >
        are treated as keys to a documentation node and are processed by the
        *note TOH: (Text)TOH, function, producing a link followed by the
        headline. In particular, links beginning with >  are treated as
        subnodes. The purpose is to allow a classification of the documentation
        nodes grouped under a subsections. Further indentation is treated as
        further sub-classifications.
      * Example -- examples of the usage

        The text in this subsection consists of lines of Macaulay2 code to be
        used as examples in the documentation node. Output will be generated
        automatically and incorporated into the display.
      * CannedExample -- pre-evaluated example results

        The text in this subsection consists of lines containing an interaction
        with Macaulay2, to displayed as an example.
      * Pre -- preformatted text

        The text in this subsection, with common indentation removed, is
        wrapped with *note PRE: (Text)PRE,.
      * Code -- custom Macaulay2 code

        The text in this subsection, with common indentation removed, is
        wrapped with parentheses and evaluated; the result is spliced into the
        list of documentation items at the appropriate point before passing the
        list to *note document: (Macaulay2Doc)document,.
  * Synopsis -- prepare documentation for different usages of a function

    Each section with this keyword prepares a standardized synopsis similar to
    the *note SYNOPSIS: (Macaulay2Doc)SYNOPSIS, template of the classic *note
    document: (Macaulay2Doc)document, command. The text in this section occurs
    in subsections with the following keywords:

      * *note Heading: (Macaulay2Doc)SYNOPSIS, -- similar to the *note
        Headline: (Macaulay2Doc)Headline, keyword
      * *note BaseFunction: (Macaulay2Doc)BaseFunction, -- the basename of the
        function; see *note SYNOPSIS(...,BaseFunction=>...):
        (Macaulay2Doc)SYNOPSIS,
      * Keywords that can be repeated once for each synopsis: *note Usage:
        (Macaulay2Doc)Usage, (required), *note Inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)Inputs,,
        *note Outputs: (Macaulay2Doc)Outputs,, *note Consequences:
        (Macaulay2Doc)Consequences,, and Description.

  * *note Acknowledgement: (Macaulay2Doc)Acknowledgement, -- acknowledgements
    listed in a documentation node
    *note Contributors: (Macaulay2Doc)Contributors, -- non-author contributors
    listed in a documentation node
    *note References: (Macaulay2Doc)References, -- references listed in a
    documentation node*note Citation: (Macaulay2Doc)Citation, -- package
    citation information

    The text in these sections is parsed and rendered similar to the *note
    Text: (Text)Top, subsection and can be used to acknowledge funding sources,
    collaborators, and non-author contributors, or to provide references or a
    preferred style citing the package in academic papers. You can also use the
    Tree keyword in the first line of this section and use the nested tree
    structure described above.
  * *note Caveat: (Macaulay2Doc)Caveat, -- warnings or limitations listed in a
    documentation node

    The text in this section is parsed and rendered similar to the *note Text:
    (Text)Top, subsection and can be used to inform users of potential limits
    of the implementation.
  * *note SeeAlso: (Macaulay2Doc)SeeAlso, -- cross-references to other
    documentation nodes

    The lines in this section are parsed as in the *note Key:
    (Macaulay2Doc)Key, section and are displayed as links to other relevant
    documentation nodes.
  * *note Subnodes: (Macaulay2Doc)Subnodes, -- a menu of documentation nodes

    The lines in this section not beginning with a colon are keys giving links
    to the subnodes of the current documentation node. The purpose is to allow
    the documentation of the package to be arranged linearly, as in a book.
    The lines beginning with a colon are subheadings used to classify the
    subnodes.
  * *note SourceCode: (Macaulay2Doc)SourceCode, -- sources displayed in a
    documentation node

    The lines in this section are parsed as a list of functions whose source
    code should be displayed in the documentation.
  * *note ExampleFiles: (Macaulay2Doc)ExampleFiles, -- data files corresponding
    to a documentation node

    Each line in this section should be the name of a file in the auxiliary
    files directory of the package; the name of that directory is the same as
    the name of the package.  Copies of the corresponding files will be made
    available in the running path of the examples.

Markup keywords allowed in documentation strings
================================================

The documentation system allows using $\LaTeX$ commands, so mathematical
expressions may be displayed as $\PP^3$ or $x_i^{33} + 1/2$ in the html form of
the documentation. For details and examples see *note html(TEX):
(Text)html_lpTEX_rp,.

Also allowed is a pair of @ characters, enclosing Macaulay2 code to be
executed, yielding appropriate hypertext.  For example "The @TO Key@ section is
required" will appear as "The *note Key: (Macaulay2Doc)Key, section is
required." This package also provides a number of helper functions, such as the
*note arXiv: arXiv, function, which may be used to succinctly reference
e-Prints. See *note Text: (Text)Top, for other hypertext types.

Note that any Macaulay2 code resulting in a *note String: (Macaulay2Doc)String,
or *note Hypertext: (Text)Hypertext, may be used inside @...@, but for longer
code it is easier to use the Code keyword described above.



Documenting multiple nodes at once
==================================

If the least indented keyword in the documentation string s is Node, possibly
occurring multiple times, multiple documentation nodes will be prepared.

  * Node

    The text following each such keyword is handled just as by *note doc: doc,
    resulting in a separate documentation node.

See SimpleDoc/doc.txt (see common/share/Macaulay2/SimpleDoc/doc.txt ) for an
example of how the Node keyword is used in documenting this package.

